.TH ARES_DESTROY 3 "7 December 2004"
.SH NAME
ares_destroy \- Destroy a resolver channel
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ares.h>

void ares_destroy(ares_channel_t *\fIchannel\fP)
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fBares_destroy(3)\fP function destroys the name service channel
identified by \fIchannel\fP, freeing all memory and closing all sockets used
by the channel.

\fBares_destroy(3)\fP invokes the callbacks for each pending query on the
channel, passing a status of \fIARES_EDESTRUCTION\fP. These calls give the
callbacks a chance to clean up any state which might have been stored in their
arguments. A callback must not add new requests to a channel being destroyed.
Such a request is refused: the request is not started, and its callback is
invoked with a status of \fIARES_EDESTRUCTION\fP so that any state attached to
its argument can be released. A request made from within that notification is
refused without a further notification, so that a callback which responds to
failure by retrying cannot recurse without bound; its argument is not handed
back. This applies as of c-ares version 1.35.0, and c-ares version 1.34.9 on
the 1.34 branch.

As of c-ares 1.35.0, \fBares_destroy(3)\fP may be called from within a
callback invoked by the channel.  In that case the channel cannot be torn down
immediately, as the callback stack still references it, so
\fBares_destroy(3)\fP returns without destroying it and the teardown is
deferred until the callback returns.  The channel must not be used again after
such a call.  Prior versions explicitly did not support this and doing so
resulted in undefined behavior (including a deadlock when using an event
thread).

Because the teardown is deferred, the callbacks of other queries still pending
on the channel are invoked after \fBares_destroy(3)\fP has returned: some may
complete or fail in the same processing pass, and the rest receive
\fIARES_EDESTRUCTION\fP when the teardown runs.  Any state those callbacks
reference through their argument must remain valid until they have been
invoked.

Where the deferred teardown completes depends on which thread ran the callback:
.RS 4
.IP \(bu 2
If the callback was invoked from within a c-ares function the application
called, such as \fBares_process_fds(3)\fP, \fBares_process(3)\fP,
\fBares_cancel(3)\fP, \fBares_set_servers_csv(3)\fP, or
\fBares_getaddrinfo(3)\fP answering from the hosts file or a literal address,
the teardown completes before that function returns.
.IP \(bu 2
If the callback was invoked on a thread internal to c-ares, either the event
thread of a channel created with \fIARES_OPT_EVENT_THREAD\fP or the thread
\fBares_reinit(3)\fP starts to reload the system configuration, the teardown
completes on that thread some time after the callback returns.  There is no
way to wait for it; \fBares_queue_wait_empty(3)\fP must not be used for this,
as it reads the channel being freed.  The application must not call
\fBares_library_cleanup(3)\fP, unload the c-ares library, or exit while such a
teardown may still be running.
.RE

There is no ability to make this function thread-safe.  No additional calls
using this channel may be made once this function is called.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ares_init (3),
.BR ares_cancel (3),
.BR ares_library_cleanup (3),
.BR ares_queue_wait_empty (3),
.BR ares_reinit (3),
.BR ares_threadsafety (3)
